<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Suspected demyelinating disease in a 28-year-old professional cellist with three weeks of numbness, loss of position sense and clumsiness in the fingers of both hands, worse on the left, Lhermitte&apos;s sign and fatigue, after a similar month of finger tingling 18 months ago. MRI of the brain and cervical and thoracic spine with gadolinium is booked this week. Please see her within two weeks to review the MRI with her, confirm or exclude multiple sclerosis, and plan treatment. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Urgent, within two weeks. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Does she have multiple sclerosis by current criteria, and what further tests are needed, including tests for mimics?</item><item>Should the current episode be treated, and how?</item><item>Which disease-modifying therapy do you recommend, and why, given her age, activity, travel and wish for children in future?</item><item>What screening and vaccinations are needed before treatment, and what monitoring should primary care share?</item><item>What can be done for her hand function and fatigue so that she can keep playing and teaching?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Three weeks of numbness and tingling in the fingers of both hands, worse on the left (her fingering hand); she cannot feel the strings properly, loses her place on the fingerboard and has dropped her bow; an electric sensation down the spine on bending the neck; tired by mid-afternoon for months. Eighteen months ago a month of finger tingling was put down to overwork and settled. No visual, bladder or gait symptoms. Weight 63.5 kg; blood pressure 112/70. Reduced vibration and position sense in the fingers, worse on the left; nine-hole peg test 26.0 seconds right and 33.5 seconds left. Results today: hemoglobin 13.4 g/dL, lymphocytes 1.9, platelets 246; creatinine 0.72 mg/dL; glucose 86 mg/dL; ALT 16 and AST 19 U/L; vitamin B12 468 pg/mL; TSH 1.6 mIU/L. No medicines; no known allergies. Never smoker. Married; uses condoms; not planning a pregnancy this year. She is a professional cellist who has cancelled most concerts this season and wants to know whether she will play again [S]</paragraph><list><item>For three weeks the fingers of both hands have been numb and tingling, worse on the left; she cannot feel the strings properly, loses her place on the fingerboard and has dropped her bow twice; an electric feeling runs down her back when she bends her head; tired by mid-afternoon for months. [S]</item><item>A similar month of tingling in her fingers 18 months ago was put down to overwork and stress, and settled. [S]</item><item>She wants to know what is wrong, and whether she will be able to play again. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Jacqueline Mary du Pré was born in Oxford, England, on 26 January 1945. Her mother was a pianist who had studied at the Royal Academy of Music, and her father an accountant. [D]</item><item>She began the cello as a small child, studied with William Pleeth at the Guildhall School of Music from the mid-1950s, won the Guilhermina Suggia Award at 11 in 1956 and the Queen&apos;s Prize at 15, attended a Pablo Casals masterclass in Zermatt in 1960, and made her formal debut at Wigmore Hall in London in March 1961, at 16. [D]</item><item>In 1965, at 20, she recorded Elgar&apos;s Cello Concerto with the London Symphony Orchestra under Sir John Barbirolli; she played chiefly on the Davidov Stradivarius of 1712. [D]</item><item>She converted to Judaism and married her husband, a pianist and conductor, in Jerusalem in June 1967. [D]</item><item>In 1971 her playing declined as she began to lose sensitivity in her fingers and other parts of her body; she returned to England exhausted, took a sabbatical from 1971 to 1972 and performed only rarely. When she resumed in 1972 the numbness in her fingers worsened, with fatigue. [D]</item><item>Two physicians writing about her illness note episodes of blurred vision in her teens, which they read as possibly an early sign of multiple sclerosis. [R]</item><item>Her last public concerts were in February 1973, in London, where she played the Elgar Concerto, and in New York. [D]</item></list><paragraph>5 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

